    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-01",
      "block_type": "definition",
      "heading": "Environmental effects of agriculture",
      "text": "Agriculture can damage the resources on which it depends when extraction, disturbance or waste exceeds what the setting can support. Relevant effects include erosion, overgrazing, salinisation, bush encroachment and pollution. These are different processes with different evidence and remedies. A bare field does not establish every form of degradation, and a management label does not prove that soil or water condition has improved.",
