      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-04",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Wind erosion is especially relevant where dry, loose soil lacks protective cover. Strong winds can remove finer particles, damage seedlings or deposit sand elsewhere. Suitable cover, windbreaks and reduced exposure can help, but a windbreak's effect depends on its design and maintenance. A photograph of dust does not establish the total amount lost from a particular field. Measurements or repeated observations are needed to quantify change.",
