    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-05",
      "block_type": "worked_application",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Overstocking means keeping more animals than the available resource can support over the relevant period. Overgrazing occurs when grazing removes vegetation faster than it can recover under the conditions. The terms are related but not identical. Repeated heavy use at a water point can overgraze a local area even when the whole farm's average stocking appears moderate. Recovery time, seasonal growth and the spatial distribution of animals all matter. In Namibia's northern communal areas, new boreholes allowed livestock numbers to rise, and heavy grazing around water points has stripped grass cover. Large areas of woodland in Kavango West and Kavango East have also been cleared for crops. Together with bush encroachment on rangeland, these practices contribute to desertification.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-2",
