    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-06",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Removal of grass cover exposes soil and can reduce infiltration or increase runoff where compaction and surface conditions are unfavourable. Trampling may further disturb the soil. Reduced cover and soil loss can then weaken future pasture growth, reinforcing the problem. The explanation is a chain through vegetation and soil; saying cattle cause drought confuses a management pressure with rainfall failure. Drought can lower carrying capacity and make a previously supportable stocking level excessive.",
      "claim_ids": [
