    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-09",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "High salt concentration can make plant water uptake more difficult even when soil contains water. Sensitive crops may grow poorly or die, reducing yield. Salinisation is therefore not simply the soil being dry, and it differs from waterlogging, although the two can be connected through poor drainage. Appropriate management depends on water quality, soil, drainage and crop tolerance. Applying extra irrigation without a suitable drainage outlet can make the water-table problem worse.",
