    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Bush encroachment involves an increase in woody vegetation that changes the balance with grasses in rangeland. In Namibia it is a relevant issue for grazing management. Its causes can include interacting grazing, fire, rainfall and other ecological influences, rather than one universal cause. Heavy use of grasses can reduce their competitive influence, while changes in fire patterns can favour woody establishment in some settings. Interpret the actual vegetation and management evidence.",
      "claim_ids": [
