    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-11",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Dense woody cover can reduce accessible grass forage and affect livestock carrying capacity. Some woody plants also provide browse, shade, habitat or useful biomass, so clearing every tree is not a sound definition of restoration. A management aim might be to restore a suitable grass–wood balance while retaining ecological functions. Appropriate thinning needs selectivity, follow-up and monitoring of regrowth and ground cover, because removing stems once does not establish lasting recovery.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-01-1",
