    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-13",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Agricultural pollution can arise from nutrients, pesticides, animal waste, fuel or other materials entering the wider environment in harmful quantities or forms. Nutrient runoff and leaching connect field application with water quality. Too much available nutrient can support excessive plant or algal growth; decay then consumes oxygen, potentially harming aquatic life. The causal chain needs the movement to water and the oxygen effect, not a vague claim that fertiliser poisons everything.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-01-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-01-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-02-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-02-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-03-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-03-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-04-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-04-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-05-1",
