    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-2.2.12-17",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "The scale of an environmental observation matters. Bare soil around one water point does not prove that an entire district is equally degraded. A grass-cover increase at a managed plot does not establish improvement across all farms. Compare observations at matched locations and seasons, accounting for rainfall as well as management. Repeated measurements can help distinguish a temporary response from a longer trend. A judgement should stay within the spatial and temporal extent of its evidence.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-2",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-01-1",
        "CL-6137-2.2.12-01-2",
